  {
   "id": "--RtNw0wCgAJ",
   "from": "Rachael Coon",
   "author": "23638920fc7ebd5e",
   "date": "2019-10-07T18:45:39Z",
   "subject": "lme function",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;m trying to use the lme function to run a mixed effects model.<div><br></div><div>My random effect variable is Animal_ID, my fixed effects are TrialDay, Trt, and the interaction of TrialDay and Trt. The outcome variable is duration of the Behavior, which is currently subset to Latencytoapproach.</div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/davis-rug/attach/a300d376de4fb/Annotation%202019-10-07%20113958.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"Annotation 2019-10-07 113958.png\" width=\"400\" height=\"103\" border=\"0\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Here&#39;s how I currently have my model set up:</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">LA &lt;- lme(duration ~ Trt + TrialDay + Trt*Trialday, random = Animal_ID)<br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">But I&#39;m receiving an error message:</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Error in reStruct(random, REML = REML, data = NULL) : </p><p style=\"clear:both\">  object &#39;Animal_ID&#39; not found</p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">What am I doing wrong? Please help! I&#39;m new to mixed models in R.</p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Thank you,</p><p style=\"clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"clear:both\">Rachael</p><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ZTlEarjVBAAJ",
   "from": "Evan Eskew",
   "author": "05bda608df45c6f4",
   "date": "2019-10-07T18:59:04Z",
   "subject": "Re: [D-RUG]: lme function",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Rachael,<div><br></div><div>There are a few things going on here:</div><div><br></div><div>- I believe the function you&#39;re looking to use is &quot;lmer()&quot;, not &quot;lme()&quot;</div><div>- You need to explicitly specify your data within the function call (I&#39;m going to assume your data frame is called &quot;my_data_frame&quot;)</div><div>- If, as your random effects, you&#39;re looking to have varying intercepts by &quot;Animal_ID&quot;, you&#39;ll need to specify those like so in your model formula: &quot;(1|Animal_ID)&quot;</div><div><br></div><div>Thus, I think this should work for you:</div><div><br></div><div>LA &lt;- lmer(duration ~ Trt + TrialDay + Trt*Trialday + (1|Animal_ID), data = my_data_frame)<br></div><div><br></div><div>From there, I&#39;d recommend checking out the example code here: <a href=\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/lme4/vignettes/lmer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/lme4/vignettes/lmer.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465694451000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2AHEjEX5vDKTl36H1KhWaT\">https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/lme4/vignettes/lmer.pdf</a></div><div><br></div><div>All my best,</div><div>Evan</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Oct 7, 2019 at 2:45 PM Rachael Coon &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rec...@ucdavis.edu</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
