    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Evan,<div><br></div><div>Thank you for responding. I&#39;ve referenced the dataframe and the random effects variable correctly now based on your suggestions. I spoke with a labmate who pointed out that the lmer() function does not provide p-values, which I need for my repeated measures design. Here&#39;s what she suggested:</div><div><br></div><div><div>LA &lt;- lme(duration ~ Trt + TrialDay + Trt*TrialDay, random=~1|Animal_ID, data = temp3)</div><div>anova(LA)</div><div><br></div><div>For which I got the following output:</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><a style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/davis-rug/attach/a339603901023/Annotation%202019-10-07%20124636.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"Annotation 2019-10-07 124636.png\" width=\"400\" height=\"216\" border=\"0\"></a><br></div><br><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div style=\"text-align:left\"><br></div><div>This is what I was looking for, but if you have alternative reasons for using lmer(), I&#39;m opening to hearing them.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks again,</div><div><br></div><div>Rachael</div><div><br></div><div></div></div>"
    ],
